speechtext:
I move that this clause be struck out and the following substituted therefor:-
If in any prosecution under this Act any question shall arise in respect of the matters hereinafter mentioned the burden of proof shall be upon the person charged to establish by satisfactory evidence-
(a) that he does not come within any specified class palled out;
(h) that he has duly reported in accordance with section 4 ;
(c)	that he comes within any of the exceptions set out in the schedule hereto;
(d)	that he has been duly exempted under the provisions of section 11.
And in the absence of such evidence the contrary shall he conclusively presumed.
This is much the same as the original section, but I think better expressed. It will be apparent at a glance that the proof in each case would he very readily at hand on the part of the person charged, but would be practically impossible for the prosecution to secure. For instance, the fact of -a man not coming within the class alleged de-
pends upon his age, and whether he is a British subject. Now, he knows his age; that ean he very easily established, -but it would be a very difficult matter for the prosecution to establish. With regard to coming within the exceptions, it is a principle -very frequently adopted in law that the exception must he proved by the party claiming it. Again, it is very easy for a man to prove that he has been exempted by producing a certificate, -but it would be very difficult for the prosecution to show that he had not been exempted. The burden of proof is also -on the person charged to show that he has reported-. If the bruden of proof was on the Crown they -might have to search the records of every reporting office in Canada, but the man can prove it very easily by producing evidence of it. So I think it is not only fair, but necessary, that the clause should read as amended.
